## Why This Is Asked

Designing a multi-tenant scheduler that preserves per-tenant ordering while adapting quotas to burstiness tests deep knowledge of backpressure, fairness, and liveness. It also challenges how to measure and guarantee fairness across tenants in a shared resource.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant FIFO queues
- Global bounded backlog with backpressure signaling
- Dynamic quotas via token buckets adjusted by observed burstiness
- Scheduling policy (weighted RR) to prevent starvation
- Observability: per-tenant throughput, latency, and starvation windows

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: per-tenant queues and central dispatcher
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst){ this.rate = rate; this.burst = burst; this.tokens = burst; this.last = Date.now(); }
  tryConsume(n=1){ const now = Date.now(); const elapsed = (now - this.last)/1000; this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed*this.rate); this.last = now; if(this.tokens >= n){ this.tokens -= n; return true; } return false; }
}

class TenantQueue {
  constructor(id){ this.id = id; this.q = []; }
  enqueue(item){ this.q.push(item); }
  dequeue(){ return this.q.shift(); }
}

// Dispatcher sketch (async flow-control omitted for brevity)
```

